 id:"ENG008-RS-T06",title:"Do Real-Time Bus Crowding Displays Change Boarding Choices?",genre:"transport-survey",
 text:`A city transit agency tested real-time crowding information on three bus routes. The mobile app showed each approaching bus as low, medium or high occupancy using automatic passenger-count data. Researchers wanted to know whether riders would wait for a later bus when the first one appeared crowded.

For six weeks, half of registered app users on the routes saw the crowding indicator and half saw the normal arrival-time screen. Boarding records were linked anonymously to app sessions when users had opted into research tracking.

Riders who saw a “high” crowding label were slightly more likely to let that bus pass when another bus was due within eight minutes. The effect was much smaller when the next bus was more than fifteen minutes away. This suggests that information matters most when travellers have a practical alternative.

The indicator also changed expectations. Survey respondents who saw the labels reported fewer surprises about crowded conditions, even when they boarded the first bus anyway. Satisfaction rose slightly among these users despite little change in average journey time. The system was imperfect. Passenger counts occasionally lagged after large groups boarded or left, so a bus could appear more or less crowded than it actually was. Researchers excluded sessions where the data feed had obvious faults, but smaller inaccuracies remained possible.

Another limitation is selection. Only app users who opted into tracking could be linked to individual boarding choices. Riders who use cash, do not carry smartphones or ignore the app may respond differently. The agency will next test platform displays at major stops so non-app users receive the same information. It will also compare whether crowding labels redistribute passengers enough to make loads more even across closely spaced buses.

Researchers caution against interpreting the pilot as evidence that information creates capacity. If every bus is crowded, a label cannot produce an empty seat. The value of the system lies in helping riders use existing alternatives more effectively when those alternatives are actually available.

The next analysis will also separate riders making discretionary trips from those travelling to fixed-time work or appointments. A traveller with a flexible arrival time may respond to a crowding warning differently from someone who cannot risk being late. Researchers will compare route frequency, trip purpose and waiting tolerance so that a small average effect does not hide much larger behavioural differences between groups with different constraints.

The agency also compared stops with different service frequencies. At high-frequency stops, riders responded more strongly to crowding information because another bus was usually close behind. At low-frequency stops, the same label changed expectations but rarely changed boarding. This supports the idea that information and service design interact: a useful signal has more behavioural value when the network offers a realistic alternative.`,
