 id:"ENG008-RS-C05",title:"How Shoppers Interpret Unit-Price Labels",genre:"consumer-survey",
 text:`A consumer research group studied whether clearer unit-price labels help shoppers compare package sizes. In many stores, shelf tags show both the total price and a smaller price per kilogram or litre, but the unit information is often difficult to notice.

Researchers created a simulated grocery aisle with twenty common products. Four hundred adults were randomly assigned to see either the standard shelf tag or a redesigned tag that displayed the unit price in larger type directly beneath the total price.

Participants completed twelve comparison tasks. They were asked which of two packages offered the lower unit cost, not which package had the lower total price. Accuracy was higher in the redesigned-label group, especially when packages differed greatly in size.

The benefit was smaller for participants who already reported using unit prices frequently. Their baseline accuracy was relatively high even with the standard labels. The largest gains appeared among shoppers who said they usually compared only total prices. Speed also changed. The redesigned group answered comparison questions somewhat faster, although researchers cautioned that a simulated aisle lacks the distractions and time pressure of real shopping.

After the tasks, participants rated both label formats. Most preferred the redesigned version, but some said the larger unit price made the shelf look visually crowded. This creates a design trade-off because stores also need space for promotions, loyalty prices and legally required information.

The study did not examine whether shoppers would actually buy different products or spend less money over time. Correctly identifying the lower unit price is only one step in a purchase decision; quality, brand preference and household needs still matter. Researchers plan an in-store trial using anonymised sales data and brief exit surveys. They will test whether clearer unit-price presentation changes real comparisons without confusing shoppers about the amount they pay at checkout.

The in-store phase will also test products sold in different units, such as price per 100 grams, per kilogram and per litre. Researchers want to know whether a larger label helps only when the comparison unit is already familiar or whether standardising the displayed unit produces an additional benefit. They will also track whether shoppers notice temporary promotions that make the lower total price differ from the lower unit price, since those situations require more careful interpretation. Researchers will record whether shoppers actually use the unit comparison before choosing.

Researchers also tested whether the redesigned labels created mistakes when shoppers compared products sold in different units. A small subset of tasks deliberately mixed price-per-100-grams and price-per-kilogram displays. Accuracy fell in both groups, showing that visual prominence cannot compensate for inconsistent units. The team therefore recommends that any redesign standardise the comparison unit within a product category before increasing font size or changing colour hierarchy.`,
