 id:"ENG008-RS-D07",title:"Do Retrieval Quizzes Improve Long-Term Vocabulary Recall?",genre:"digital-learning-evaluation",
 text:`An online language course tested two review methods for newly introduced vocabulary. After a lesson, learners either completed a short retrieval quiz that asked them to produce the word from a definition or viewed the same word-definition pairs again for an equal amount of time.

Eight hundred learners were randomly assigned to one of the two review methods for four weeks. Both groups studied the same number of words and received the same initial lessons. On a test given ten minutes after review, performance was similar. On a delayed test one week later, the retrieval-practice group remembered more words on average. The difference was largest for words that learners had initially answered incorrectly but then corrected after feedback.

The quiz group reported slightly higher mental effort. Some learners also said that being asked to retrieve a word before feeling ready was frustrating. However, dropout rates did not differ meaningfully between groups.

Researchers examined whether the advantage came simply from extra feedback. The restudy group also saw the correct word for the same amount of time, while the quiz group received it after each response. This design reduced, but did not entirely eliminate, differences in attention caused by actively attempting an answer.

A limitation is that the experiment measured isolated vocabulary rather than sentence production or conversation. Remembering a word on a delayed test does not guarantee fluent use in context. The platform will next test retrieval practice inside short passages and speaking tasks. Researchers also want to vary the spacing between review and test to determine how long the advantage persists.

The current result supports a specific learning claim: attempting to retrieve recently studied vocabulary can strengthen later recall compared with simply seeing the same material again, even when immediate performance looks similar.

The next experiment will also vary feedback timing. One group will see the correct answer immediately after each retrieval attempt, while another will receive feedback after a short block of questions. Researchers want to determine whether delayed feedback changes retention or frustration. They will also classify words by frequency and similarity to known vocabulary, since retrieval may help unfamiliar terms differently from words that resemble forms learners already know. This should show whether the average advantage is broad or concentrated in particular item types. Researchers will also compare response confidence.

Researchers also separated words by initial difficulty. Retrieval practice produced the largest delayed advantage for medium-difficulty items, while very easy words were remembered well in both groups and very difficult words often remained weak even after feedback. This pattern suggests that the benefit is not uniform across material. The next study will adjust item difficulty and number of retrieval attempts to test whether extra practice helps the hardest words or mainly adds frustration without durable gain.`,
